WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:04.150
You know that feeling when you have two mirrors
facing each other, and it gives the illusion

00:00:04.150 --> 00:00:07.140
of there being an infinite tunnel of rooms?

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:10.470
Or if they’re at an angle with each other,
it makes you feel like you’re part of a

00:00:10.470 --> 00:00:15.660
strange kaleidoscopic world with many copies
of yourself all separated by angled pieces

00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:17.280
of glass.

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:22.950
What many people may not realize is that the
idea underlying this illusion can be quite

00:00:22.950 --> 00:00:26.740
helpful for solving serious problems in math.

00:00:26.740 --> 00:00:34.670
We’ve already seen two videos describing
the block collision problem with it’s wonderfully

00:00:34.670 --> 00:00:36.250
surprising answer.

00:00:36.250 --> 00:00:39.950
Big block comes in from the right, lots of
clacks, the total number of clacks looks like

00:00:39.950 --> 00:00:42.800
pi, and we want to know why.

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:46.820
Here we see one more perspective explaining
what’s going on, where if the connection

00:00:46.820 --> 00:00:51.289
to pi wasn’t surprising enough we add one
more unexpected connection to optics.

00:00:51.289 --> 00:00:54.829
But we’re doing more than just answering
the same question twice.

00:00:54.829 --> 00:01:00.350
This alternate solution gives a richer understanding
that can let you answer other questions about

00:01:00.350 --> 00:01:05.279
the setup, and it’s also core to how I coded
accurate simulations of these blocks without

00:01:05.279 --> 00:01:10.020
requiring absurdly small time steps and huge
computation time.

00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:15.079
Last time we used a coordinate plane where
each point encodes a pair of velocities.

00:01:15.079 --> 00:01:21.369
Here, we use one where each point encodes
the positions of both blocks.

00:01:21.369 --> 00:01:26.229
Again, the idea is that by representing the
states of a changing system with individual

00:01:26.229 --> 00:01:31.069
points in some space, problems in dynamics
turn into problems in geometry, which are

00:01:31.069 --> 00:01:33.570
hopefully more solvable.

00:01:33.570 --> 00:01:38.770
Specifically, let the x-coordinate of a 2d
plane represent the distance from the wall

00:01:38.770 --> 00:01:42.420
to the left edge of the first block, what
I’ll call d1.

00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:47.489
And let the y-coordinate represent the distance
from the wall to the right edge of the second

00:01:47.489 --> 00:01:52.170
block, what we’ll call d2.

00:01:52.170 --> 00:01:57.950
That way, the line x=y shows us where the
two blocks clack into each other, since that

00:01:57.950 --> 00:02:01.959
happens whenever d1 equals d2.

00:02:01.959 --> 00:02:05.899
Here’s what it looks like for our scenario
to play out.

00:02:05.899 --> 00:02:10.319
As the two distances of our blocks change,
the two-dimensional point of our configuration

00:02:10.319 --> 00:02:15.280
space moves around, with a position that always
fully encodes the information of those two

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:16.660
distances.

00:02:16.660 --> 00:02:21.330
You may notice that at the bottom there, its
bounded by the line where d2 is the same as

00:02:21.330 --> 00:02:27.500
the small block’s width, which is what it
means for the small block to hit the wall.

00:02:27.500 --> 00:02:31.760
You might be able to guess where we’re going
with this: The way this point bounces between

00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:39.840
these two bounding lines is a bit like light
bouncing between mirrors.

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:42.180
The analogy doesn’t quite work, though.

00:02:42.180 --> 00:02:47.609
In the lingo of optics, the angle of incidence
doesn’t equal the angle of reflection.

00:02:47.609 --> 00:02:51.310
Just think of that first collision: A beam
of light coming in from the right would bounce

00:02:51.310 --> 00:02:59.620
off this 45-degree x=y line and end up going
straight down, which would mean only the second

00:02:59.620 --> 00:03:01.249
block is moving.

00:03:01.249 --> 00:03:05.510
This does happen in the simplest case, where
the second block has the same mass as the

00:03:05.510 --> 00:03:11.609
first and picks up all of the momentum like
a croquet ball, but for other mass ratios

00:03:11.609 --> 00:03:16.459
that first block will keep moving a bit, so
the trajectory of our point in this configuration

00:03:16.459 --> 00:03:21.500
space won’t be pointed straight down.

00:03:21.500 --> 00:03:26.230
Even if it’s not immediately clear why this
analogy with light would be helpful, and trust

00:03:26.230 --> 00:03:30.769
me it will be helpful in many ways, run with
me and see if we can fix it for the general

00:03:30.769 --> 00:03:31.780
case.

00:03:31.780 --> 00:03:36.010
Seeking analogies in math is very often a
good idea.

00:03:36.010 --> 00:03:39.269
As with last video, it’s helpful to rescale
the coordinates.

00:03:39.269 --> 00:03:44.920
In fact, motivated by what we did then, you
might think to rescale the coordinates so

00:03:44.920 --> 00:03:51.700
that x=sqrt(the first mass, m1) * d1.

00:03:51.700 --> 00:03:56.390
This has the effect of stretching our space
horizontally, so changes to our big block’s

00:03:56.390 --> 00:04:00.260
position now result in larger changes to the
x-coordinate of our point.

00:04:00.260 --> 00:04:05.870
Likewise, let’s write the y-coordinate as
sqrt(m2) * d2, even though in this particular

00:04:05.870 --> 00:04:10.889
case that second mass is 1, so it doesn’t
matter.

00:04:10.889 --> 00:04:15.450
Maybe that strikes you as making things uglier,
and somewhat of a random thing to do, but

00:04:15.450 --> 00:04:20.880
as with last time, including square roots
of masses like this plays more nicely with

00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:23.560
the laws of conserving energy and momentum.

00:04:23.560 --> 00:04:28.060
Specifically, the conservation of energy will
translate to the fact that our little point

00:04:28.060 --> 00:04:32.520
in configuration space is always moving at
the same speed, which in our analogy you might

00:04:32.520 --> 00:04:35.060
think of as meaning there’s a constant speed
of light.

00:04:35.060 --> 00:04:39.710
And the conservation of momentum will translate
to the fact that as our point bounces off

00:04:39.710 --> 00:04:44.349
the “mirrors” of our setup, so to speak,
the angle of incidence equals the angle of

00:04:44.349 --> 00:04:45.669
reflection.

00:04:45.669 --> 00:04:49.750
Doesn’t that seem bizarre?

00:04:49.750 --> 00:04:54.030
That the laws of mechanics should translate
to laws of optics like this?

00:04:54.030 --> 00:04:59.229
To see why this is true, let’s roll up our
sleeves and work out the math.

00:04:59.229 --> 00:05:03.580
Focus on is the velocity vector for our point
in the diagram, showing which direction its

00:05:03.580 --> 00:05:05.289
moving and how quickly.

00:05:05.289 --> 00:05:10.560
Note, this is not a physical velocity, like
the velocities of the moving blocks; instead

00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:15.669
it’s a more abstract rate of change in the
context of our configuration space, whose

00:05:15.669 --> 00:05:23.630
two dimensions worth of possible directions
encodes both velocities of the blocks.

00:05:23.630 --> 00:05:30.210
The x-component of this little vector is the
rate of change of x.

00:05:30.210 --> 00:05:34.419
Likewise, its y-component is the rate of change
for y.

00:05:34.419 --> 00:05:38.250
So what is the rate of change for the x-coordinate?

00:05:38.250 --> 00:05:47.110
Well, x is sqrt(m1)*d1, and the mass doesn’t
change, so it depends only on how d1 changes.

00:05:47.110 --> 00:05:53.080
The rate at which d1 changes is the velocity
of the big block, what I’ll call v1.

00:05:53.080 --> 00:06:00.319
Likewise, the rate of change for y is sqrt(m2)*v2.

00:06:00.319 --> 00:06:06.100
Now notice what the magnitude of our little
configuration-space-change-vector is: Using

00:06:06.100 --> 00:06:10.410
the pythagorean theorem, it’s the square
root of the sum each of these component rates

00:06:10.410 --> 00:06:17.530
of change squared, which is the sqrt(m1*v1^2
+ m2*v2^2).

00:06:17.530 --> 00:06:26.020
That inner expression is twice the kinetic
energy, so the speed of our point in configuration

00:06:26.020 --> 00:06:31.340
space is some function of the total energy,
which stays constant throughout the process.

00:06:31.340 --> 00:06:38.539
Remember, a core assumption here is that no
energy lost to friction or to the collisions.

00:06:38.539 --> 00:06:45.009
Alright, that’s cool, with these rescaled
coordinates our little point always moves

00:06:45.009 --> 00:06:46.009
with a constant speed.

00:06:46.009 --> 00:06:50.509
I know it’s not obvious why you’d care,
but among other things it’s important for

00:06:50.509 --> 00:06:55.009
the next step, where the conservation of momentum
implies that these two bounding lines act

00:06:55.009 --> 00:06:57.250
like mirrors.

00:06:57.250 --> 00:07:07.180
First, notice that the line for d1=d2, representing
where blocks collide, is no longer the 45-degree

00:07:07.180 --> 00:07:08.250
x=y line.

00:07:08.250 --> 00:07:19.460
Instead, after a little rearrangement, see
see it looks like the line x/sqrt(m1) = y/sqrt(m2)

00:07:19.460 --> 00:07:22.880
which is a line with slope sqrt(m2 / m1).

00:07:22.880 --> 00:07:30.530
That’s a nice expression to tuck away in
the back of your mind.

00:07:30.530 --> 00:07:34.680
After the blocks collide, meaning our point
hits this line, the way to figure out how

00:07:34.680 --> 00:07:42.750
they move is to use the conservation of momentum,
which says the value m1*v1 + m2*v2 remains

00:07:42.750 --> 00:07:45.180
unchanged after the collision.

00:07:45.180 --> 00:07:52.580
Notice, this looks like a dot product between
two column vectors, [m1, m2], and [v1, v2].

00:07:52.580 --> 00:07:57.121
Rewriting it slightly for our rescaled coordinates,
the same thing could be written as a dot product

00:07:57.121 --> 00:08:02.200
between a column vector with the square roots
of the masses, and one with the rates of change

00:08:02.200 --> 00:08:03.719
for x and y.

00:08:03.719 --> 00:08:09.659
I know this probably seems like a complicated
way to talk about a simple momentum equation,

00:08:09.659 --> 00:08:14.789
but there is a good reason for shifting to
a language of dot products in our new coordinates.

00:08:14.789 --> 00:08:20.460
Notice, that second one is the rate of change
vector for the point in our diagram.

00:08:20.460 --> 00:08:26.689
The key is that this square-root-of-the-masses
vector points in the same direction as our

00:08:26.689 --> 00:08:32.170
collision line, since its rise over run is
sqrt(m2) over sqrt(m1).

00:08:32.170 --> 00:08:36.540
If you’re unfamiliar with the dot product,
you can find another video on this channel

00:08:36.540 --> 00:08:40.650
describing it, but real quick let’s review
what it means geometrically.

00:08:40.650 --> 00:08:46.360
The dot product of two vectors equals the
length of the first, time the length of the

00:08:46.360 --> 00:08:52.640
projection of the second one onto that first,
considered negative if they point in opposite

00:08:52.640 --> 00:08:53.640
directions.

00:08:53.640 --> 00:08:58.290
You often see this written as the product
of the lengths of each vector, and the cosine

00:08:58.290 --> 00:09:01.980
of the angle between them.

00:09:01.980 --> 00:09:08.500
Alright, so look back at this conservation
of momentum expression, telling us that the

00:09:08.500 --> 00:09:14.640
dot product between this square-roots-of-the-masses
vector with our change vector is the same

00:09:14.640 --> 00:09:17.380
before and after the collision.

00:09:17.380 --> 00:09:22.000
Since we just saw that this change vector
has a constant magnitude, the only way for

00:09:22.000 --> 00:09:26.970
this dot product to stay the same is if the
angle it makes with that collision line stays

00:09:26.970 --> 00:09:28.270
the same.

00:09:28.270 --> 00:09:33.250
In other words, again using the lingo of optics,
the angle of incidence and the angle of reflection

00:09:33.250 --> 00:09:36.100
off this collision line must be equal.

00:09:36.100 --> 00:09:40.480
Similarly, when the small block bounces off
the wall, our little vector gets reflected

00:09:40.480 --> 00:09:47.881
about the x-direction, and our configuration
point bounces off a horizontal line just as

00:09:47.881 --> 00:09:50.650
a beam of light would, since only its y-component
changes

00:09:50.650 --> 00:09:54.940
So, stepping back, what this means is that
our original question of counting block collisions,

00:09:54.940 --> 00:10:00.040
and trying to understand why on earth pi would
show, can be translated to a completely different

00:10:00.040 --> 00:10:05.070
question: If you shine a beam of light at
a pair of mirrors, meeting each other at some

00:10:05.070 --> 00:10:12.070
angle theta, how many times will that light
bounce off the mirrors, as a function of that

00:10:12.070 --> 00:10:13.070
angle?

00:10:13.070 --> 00:10:19.450
Remember, the mass ratio of our blocks determines
this angle theta in the analogy.

00:10:19.450 --> 00:10:24.350
Now I can hear some of you complaining: “Haven’t
we replaced one tricky setup with another?”.

00:10:24.350 --> 00:10:27.370
This might make for a cute analogy, but how
is it progress?

00:10:27.370 --> 00:10:31.520
It’s true, counting the number of light
bounces here is hard.

00:10:31.520 --> 00:10:34.040
But now we have a helpful trick:

00:10:34.040 --> 00:10:38.180
When the beam of light hits the mirror, instead
of thinking of the beam as reflecting about

00:10:38.180 --> 00:10:43.250
the mirror, think of the beam as going straight
while the whole world gets flipped.

00:10:43.250 --> 00:10:47.750
It’s as if the beam is passing through a
piece of glass into an illustory “looking

00:10:47.750 --> 00:10:49.600
glass universe.”

00:10:49.600 --> 00:10:51.540
Think of actual mirrors here.

00:10:51.540 --> 00:10:55.640
This wire on the left will represent a laser
beam coming into the mirror, while the one

00:10:55.640 --> 00:10:58.250
on the right will represent its reflection.

00:10:58.250 --> 00:11:02.940
The illusion is that the beam goes straight
through the mirror, as if passing through

00:11:02.940 --> 00:11:05.760
a window separating us from another room.

00:11:05.760 --> 00:11:06.760
But notice!

00:11:06.760 --> 00:11:07.760
Crucially!

00:11:07.760 --> 00:11:13.210
For this illusion to work the angle of incidence
has to equal the angle of reflection.

00:11:13.210 --> 00:11:19.000
Otherwise the flipped copy of the reflected
beam won’t line up with the first part.

00:11:19.000 --> 00:11:23.370
So all that work we did rescaling coordinates
and futzing through the momentum equation

00:11:23.370 --> 00:11:25.250
was certainly necessary.

00:11:25.250 --> 00:11:29.880
But now we get to enjoy the fruits of our
labor, watch how this helps us elegantly solve

00:11:29.880 --> 00:11:39.250
the question of how many mirror bounces there
will be.

00:11:39.250 --> 00:11:43.520
Everytime the beam hits a mirror, don’t
think of the beam as getting reflected, let

00:11:43.520 --> 00:11:47.060
it continue straight while the world gets
reflected.

00:11:47.060 --> 00:11:51.850
As this goes on, the illusion to the light
beam is that instead of getting bounced around

00:11:51.850 --> 00:11:56.170
between these two angled mirrors many times,
it’s passing through a sequence of angled

00:11:56.170 --> 00:12:00.100
pieces of glass, all the same angle apart.

00:12:00.100 --> 00:12:05.190
Right now I’m showing all the reflected
copies of our bouncing beam, which I think

00:12:05.190 --> 00:12:11.570
has a very striking beauty to it.

00:12:11.570 --> 00:12:16.170
But for a clearer view, just focus on the
on the original bouncing beam, and the illusory

00:12:16.170 --> 00:12:17.540
straight one.

00:12:17.540 --> 00:12:22.430
The question of counting bounces turns into
a question of how many pieces of glass this

00:12:22.430 --> 00:12:31.440
illusory beam crosses; how many reflected
copies of the world does it pass into.

00:12:31.440 --> 00:12:40.930
Well, calling the angle between the mirrors
theta, the answer here is however many times

00:12:40.930 --> 00:12:46.120
you can add theta to itself before you get
more than halfway around the circle, which

00:12:46.120 --> 00:12:51.940
is to say more than pi total radians.

00:12:51.940 --> 00:12:57.230
Written as a formula, the answer to our question
is the floor of pi divided by theta.

00:12:57.230 --> 00:12:59.140
Let’s review!

00:12:59.140 --> 00:13:03.730
We started by drawing a configuration space
for our colliding blocks, where the x and

00:13:03.730 --> 00:13:07.180
y coordinates represented the two distances
from the wall.

00:13:07.180 --> 00:13:12.300
The kind of looked like light bouncing off
two mirrors, but to make the analogy work

00:13:12.300 --> 00:13:16.260
properly, we needed to rescale the coordinates
by the square roots of the masses.

00:13:16.260 --> 00:13:25.300
This made the slope of one line sqrt(m2) / sqrt(m1),
so the angle between our two bounding lines

00:13:25.300 --> 00:13:28.670
will be the inverse tangent of this slope.

00:13:28.670 --> 00:13:32.790
To figure out how many bounces there are between
two mirrors like this, think of the illusion

00:13:32.790 --> 00:13:36.830
of the beam continuing straight through a
sequence of looking glass universes separated

00:13:36.830 --> 00:13:39.440
by a semicircular fan of windows.

00:13:39.440 --> 00:13:44.920
The answer, then, comes down to how many times
the value of angle fits into 180-degrees,

00:13:44.920 --> 00:13:46.190
which is pi radians.

00:13:46.190 --> 00:13:51.740
From here, to understand why exactly the digits
of pi show up, when the mass ratio is a power

00:13:51.740 --> 00:13:57.550
of 100, it’s exactly what we did in the
last video, so I won’t repeat myself here.

00:13:57.550 --> 00:14:02.791
And finally, as we reflect on how absurd the
initial appearance of pi seemed, on the two

00:14:02.791 --> 00:14:08.480
solutions we’ve now seen, and on how unexpectedly
helpful it can be to represent the state of

00:14:08.480 --> 00:14:13.660
your system with points in some space, I leave
you with this quote form computer scientist

00:14:13.660 --> 00:14:17.630
Alan Kay: “A change of perspective is worth
80 IQ points”.

